+## CRITICAL Findings
+> Must fix before merge.
+
+**[C-1]: `isContentCardOfflineAvailable` defaults to `false` — inverts documented contract, silently destroys disk cache on every network response** (from: Code, HLD, LLD, Completeness, Complexity)
+- **File:** `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:1015`
+- **What:** The function evaluates `?? false` when `messaging.contentCardOfflineAvailable` is absent from the Configuration shared state. Its own doc comment two lines above states "Defaults to `true` when the key is absent (backwards-compatible: offline availability is on unless the operator explicitly disables it)." The code is the exact opposite of the documented intent.
+- **Why:** Two silent consequences: (1) In `applyPropositionChangeFor` (line 1281–1290), when `ccOfflineAvailable = false`, the code calls `clearPersistedContentCardPropositions()`, wiping `qualifiedContentCardsBySurface`, `contentCardRulesBySurface`, `contentCardOriginByProposition`, `inMemoryContentCardPropositions`, the CC rules engine, and the disk cache. Every successful network personalization response for any app that does not explicitly set the config key destroys the disk cache. Boot-time hydration will seed data, but the first network refresh after launch immediately clears it. (2) `usePersistedContentCards: true` calls in `retrieveMessages` (line 1479–1488) hit the `isContentCardOfflineAvailable` guard and return a silent empty response — indistinguishable from "no cards exist."
+- **Fix:** Change `?? false` to `?? true` at line 1015. Update the log message at line 1016 from "defaulting to false" to "defaulting to true." The `applyPropositionChangeFor` `else` branch must also be reviewed: when the feature defaults on, `clearPersistedContentCardPropositions()` should only be called when the operator explicitly sets the key to `false`, not on key absence.
+- **Confidence:** 1.0
+- **Reviewer conflict:** HLD raises the valid counter-argument that `?? false` (opt-in) may be the actual intent, in which case the doc comment and AGENTS.md summary are both wrong. Either way, code and documentation must be consistent. The HLD reviewer's own confidence that this is a critical inversion is 0.97.
